<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zhongshan</h1><p class="st-meta">506 BCE – 296 BCE (approx.) · capital Lingshou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom founded by the Di people of Hebei that rose to call itself one of China's royal powers, known for the spectacular bronzes from King Cuo's tomb.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zhongshan was founded by the Xianyu, a Baidi people who had settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains">Taihang</a> foothills of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>. Their state, known as Xianyu in Spring and Autumn sources, took the name Zhongshan in the 5th century BCE. Conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a> around 407 BCE, it regained independence around 380 BCE and moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingshou">Lingshou</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zhongshan adopted much of Chinese court culture while keeping its own customs. In 323 BCE its ruler joined four other states in proclaiming himself king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-cuo-of-zhongshan">King Cuo</a>'s tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingshan-county">Pingshan</a>, excavated in the 1970s, contained bronzes with long inscriptions, inlaid winged beasts and a bronze plan of the royal cemetery, the oldest known architectural drawing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After taking part in the invasion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-yan">State of Yan</a> in 314 BCE, Zhongshan was gradually conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wuling-of-zhao">King Wuling</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zhao">State of Zhao</a>, who completed its annexation in 296 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingshou">Lingshou</a> city walls and royal tombs survive near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingshan-county">Pingshan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zhongshan.jpg" alt="Zhongshan" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: EditQ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%81%87%E6%97%A5%E5%B9%BF%E5%9C%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>506 BCE – 296 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lingshou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>King Cuo of Zhongshan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xianyu, State of Zhongshan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-401&amp;focus=zhongshan" aria-label="Zhongshan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7713 1346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zhongshan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8033 1602L7876 1114"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7876 1114h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8033 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8033 1602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-401&amp;focus=zhongshan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

